<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

Most CAT-SOOP course pages should use `content.catsoop` or `content.md`.
Sometimes, however, the response itself is primarily programmatic: a report
depends on request arguments, a URL should redirect elsewhere, or the page
needs to return JSON, CSV, or another non-page format.  A `content.py` page is
useful for these cases.

`content.py` is trusted server-side Python.  It runs with the CAT-SOOP page
context as its global namespace and can print HTML, inspect request and user
data, call CAT-SOOP modules, or select a different response handler.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Creating a Python Page</catsoop-section>

A directory page can use `content.py` in place of `content.catsoop`:

```text
numerical_methods/
└── reports/
    ├── preload.py
    └── content.py
```

Alternatively, a single file such as `status.py` creates a page named
`status` in its containing directory.

A minimal Python page prints HTML:

```python
import html

name = cs_form.get("name", "visitor")

print("<h2>Current status</h2>")
print("<p>Hello, %s.</p>" % html.escape(name))
```

`print()` output becomes `cs_content`.  Unlike a `.catsoop` or `.md` source
file, output from `content.py` is treated as HTML rather than being converted
from Markdown.

The usual page context is available, including:

* `cs_form`, containing URL query arguments and POST fields;
* `cs_user_info` and `cs_username`, describing the current user;
* `cs_path_info`, the current path as a list beginning with the course name;
* `cs_url_root` and `cs_course`, for constructing URLs; and
* modules such as `csm_cslog`, `csm_dispatch`, and `csm_tutor`.

See [Page Rendering and Request Data](COURSE/docs/authoring/rendering) for more
about `cs_form` and the request lifecycle.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
Page Python runs inside the CAT-SOOP server process, not in a student-code
sandbox.  Treat request values as untrusted, escape values inserted into HTML,
check authorization on the server before reading or changing protected data,
and do not execute text supplied by a user.
</div>

<catsoop-section>How `cs_handler` Selects the Response</catsoop-section>

After loading the page, CAT-SOOP reads `cs_handler`.  If the page did not set
it, CAT-SOOP uses the `default` handler, which renders questions and normal
course-page behavior.

The selected handler's `handle(context)` function determines what happens
next.  If it returns ordinary content, CAT-SOOP places that content in the
normal site template.  If it returns an HTTP response tuple, CAT-SOOP sends
that response directly without applying the page template.

Common built-in choices are:

<table class="table table-striped">
<thead><tr><th><code>cs_handler</code></th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>"default"</code></td><td>Normal course pages and question handling</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>"raw_response"</code></td><td>Return exact text or bytes with a chosen media type</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>"redirect"</code></td><td>Return an HTTP 302 redirect to <code>redirect_location</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>"passthrough"</code></td><td>Pass through already-produced content without normal question handling</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

Advanced courses can define a handler at
`__HANDLERS__/NAME/NAME.py`.  CAT-SOOP looks for a course handler first and
then for a built-in handler.  Most course endpoints need only
`raw_response` or `redirect`.

<catsoop-section>Raw Responses and `content_type`</catsoop-section>

The `raw_response` handler reads three principal variables:

```python
cs_handler = "raw_response"
content_type = "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
response = "Generated directly by CAT-SOOP.\n"
```

It returns `response` without the CAT-SOOP header, breadcrumbs, footer, or page
template.  A string response is encoded as UTF-8; a bytes response is sent
unchanged.  If `content_type` is omitted, it defaults to `text/plain`.

The handler supplies HTTP 200, `Content-type`, and `Content-length` headers.
It can also make the response a browser download:

```python
cs_handler = "raw_response"
content_type = "text/csv; charset=utf-8"
response = "name,score\nAda,1.0\n"
download = True
filename = "scores.csv"
```

`download=True` adds a `Content-Disposition: attachment` header.  `filename`
controls the suggested filename.

Use a bytes response for binary formats:

```python
cs_handler = "raw_response"
content_type = "application/octet-stream"
response = generated_bytes
download = True
filename = "results.bin"
```

`content_type` is an HTTP media type, not a CAT-SOOP source format.  Typical
values include `text/html`, `application/json`, `text/csv`, `application/pdf`,
and `application/zip`.

<catsoop-section>Redirecting a Directory Page</catsoop-section>

A useful course structure is to make a section URL redirect to its calendar
entry or first child.  For example, a `homework/content.py` page can redirect
`/COURSE/homework` to the course calendar.  The following pattern generates a
small raw HTML document containing an immediate browser redirect and a
fallback link:

```python
cs_handler = "raw_response"
content_type = "text/html"

target = "/".join(
    [cs_url_root] + cs_path_info[:-1] + ["calendar"]
)

response = f"""<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0; url={target}">
  <title>Redirecting...</title>
</head>
<body>
  <p>If you are not redirected, <a href="{target}">continue to the calendar</a>.</p>
</body>
</html>"""
```

For `cs_path_info == ["numerical_methods", "homework"]`,
`cs_path_info[:-1]` is `["numerical_methods"]`, so `target` becomes the
course's `/numerical_methods/calendar` URL.

This is an HTML-level redirect: the initial response has status 200, and the
browser acts on the `meta` element.  When a fallback document is unnecessary,
the built-in redirect handler gives a true HTTP redirect:

```python
cs_handler = "redirect"
redirect_location = "/".join(
    [cs_url_root] + cs_path_info[:-1] + ["calendar"]
)
```

That version returns HTTP 302 with a `Location` header.

<catsoop-section>Short Request-Driven Endpoint Patterns</catsoop-section>

A Python page can serve normal HTML for one request and structured data for
another.  Test the request fields first, set the raw handler for endpoint
requests, and otherwise print the page.

<catsoop-subsection>Returning JSON</catsoop-subsection>

```python
import json

is_staff = cs_user_info.get("role") in {"Admin", "Instructor", "TA"}

if "status" in cs_form:
    cs_handler = "raw_response"
    content_type = "application/json"
    response = json.dumps({
        "ok": is_staff,
        "message": "ready" if is_staff else "permission denied",
    })
else:
    print("<h2>Report console</h2>")
    print('<a href="?status=1">Request JSON status</a>')
```

This dispatch pattern is also useful for an AJAX endpoint.  Always repeat the
permission check on the server; hiding a browser button does not protect the
endpoint.

<catsoop-subsection>Returning a CSV Download</catsoop-subsection>

```python
import csv
import io

is_staff = cs_user_info.get("role") in {"Admin", "Instructor", "TA"}

if "export" in cs_form and is_staff:
    output = io.StringIO(newline="")
    writer = csv.DictWriter(output, fieldnames=["name", "score"])
    writer.writeheader()
    writer.writerows([
        {"name": "Ada", "score": 1.0},
        {"name": "Grace", "score": 0.9},
    ])

    cs_handler = "raw_response"
    content_type = "text/csv; charset=utf-8"
    response = output.getvalue()
    download = True
    filename = "score-report.csv"
else:
    print("<h2>Score report</h2>")
```

For sensitive exports, return no data unless the role or permissions authorize
the request.  A larger application can keep endpoint logic in helper modules,
but the helper must update the page context's `cs_handler`, `content_type`, and
`response` values before page handling begins.

<catsoop-section>Staff-Only Instructor Notes</catsoop-section>

A `.catsoop` page can use a Python block to show instructor material only to
selected roles.  The same block can add a query-driven button that temporarily
hides the notes for projecting the page in class.

Put the following near the beginning of the page, inside a page Python block:

```python
staff_roles = {"Admin", "Instructor", "TA"}
is_staff = cs_user_info.get("role") in staff_roles
hide_instructor_notes = "hide_notes" in cs_form


def staff_note(note_html):
    """Print trusted instructor HTML only for staff."""
    if is_staff and not hide_instructor_notes:
        print(
            '<div class="instructor-note" '
            'style="border-left:4px solid #b00; padding:.5em 1em; '
            'margin:1em 0; color:#b00;">'
            '<b>Instructor note.</b> %s</div>' % note_html
        )
```

Then print the staff controls and notes:

```python
if is_staff:
    if hide_instructor_notes:
        print(
            '<form method="get">'
            '<button type="submit">Show instructor notes</button>'
            '</form>'
        )
    else:
        print(
            '<form method="get" style="display:inline">'
            '<input type="hidden" name="hide_notes" value="1">'
            '<button type="submit">Hide instructor notes</button>'
            '</form>'
        )

staff_note(
    "<b>Pacing:</b> spend ten minutes on the first example, "
    "then compare the two solution methods."
)
```

Clicking **Hide instructor notes** reloads the same path with
`?hide_notes=1`.  Because `cs_form` contains query arguments,
`hide_instructor_notes` becomes true and the helper prints no notes.  This
setting is not persistent: loading the page without `hide_notes` shows the
notes again.  The server-side `is_staff` check is what prevents students from
receiving the note HTML.

If many notes are scattered through a page, call `staff_note(...)` each time
rather than repeating the role check.  The function accepts trusted
course-authored HTML; do not pass it unescaped request data.

<catsoop-section>Letting Staff Clear Their Page Submissions</catsoop-section>

During a live demonstration, an instructor may want to reset their own answers
and run the page again from a clean state.  The default question handler stores
the current per-question state for a page in the user's `problemstate` log.
Deleting that one log resets saved answers, scores, attempt counts, locks, and
other current state for that user and page.

Add the server-side action to the same initial Python block:

```python
staff_roles = {"Admin", "Instructor", "TA"}
is_staff = cs_user_info.get("role") in staff_roles
submissions_cleared = False

if is_staff and "clear_submissions" in cs_form:
    csm_cslog.delete_log(
        cs_user_info["username"],
        cs_path_info,
        "problemstate",
    )
    submissions_cleared = True
```

Then give staff a POST button:

```python
if is_staff:
    print(
        '<form method="post" style="display:inline" '
        'onsubmit="return confirm('
        "'Clear all of your submissions on this page?');"
        '">'
        '<input type="hidden" name="clear_submissions" value="1">'
        '<button type="submit">Clear submissions</button>'
        '</form>'
    )

if submissions_cleared:
    print(
        '<strong style="color:#b00">Your submissions were cleared.</strong>'
    )
```

The complete flow is:

1. the button submits `clear_submissions=1` to the current page with POST;
2. CAT-SOOP merges that field into `cs_form`;
3. the server verifies the user's staff role;
4. `delete_log` removes only that username's `problemstate` for
   `cs_path_info`; and
5. the page reloads and the default handler sees an empty current state.

The original activity history in `problemactions` is not deleted, and manual
grading records in `problemgrades` are separate.  This makes the button useful
for resetting the visible working state without erasing the audit trail.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
Deleting <code>problemstate</code> is still a destructive action: the current
saved responses, scores, and attempt counts cannot be reconstructed from that
snapshot automatically.  Keep the confirmation prompt, use POST for the
mutation, check authorization on the server, and make the button's scope
explicit.  The example resets only the currently authenticated staff member,
not all instructors and not the class.
</div>

To place both controls in one staff panel, combine the two forms:

```python
if is_staff and not hide_instructor_notes:
    print(
        '<div class="instructor-controls" '
        'style="border:2px solid #b00; padding:.6em 1em;">'
        '<form method="get" style="display:inline">'
        '<input type="hidden" name="hide_notes" value="1">'
        '<button type="submit">Hide instructor notes</button>'
        '</form> '
        '<form method="post" style="display:inline" '
        'onsubmit="return confirm('
        "'Clear all of your submissions on this page?');"
        '">'
        '<input type="hidden" name="clear_submissions" value="1">'
        '<button type="submit">Clear submissions</button>'
        '</form>'
        '</div>'
    )
```

This version uses ordinary forms rather than requiring a separate JavaScript
file.  An asynchronous `fetch()` call can provide an in-place status message,
but the server-side role check and `csm_cslog.delete_log(...)` call remain the
same.

<catsoop-section>Choosing Between Page Styles</catsoop-section>

Use:

* `content.catsoop` for prose, questions, and occasional page Python;
* `content.py` when most of the page is generated from Python or the URL acts
  as an endpoint;
* the default handler for normal question behavior;
* `raw_response` for an exact body and media type; and
* `redirect` for an HTTP 302.

Keep dynamic endpoints small.  Move reusable business logic into a helper
module, keep request dispatch and response selection visible near the top of
the page, and test the HTML and non-HTML response paths independently.
